title op6: one name lost on both sides, with gains that disagree

# VR8: the one-move rule of Definition 9.3, restated on deltas.  Set
# succession removes the antecedent labels, so the rule has to be
# expressible without them, and this is the shape that tests whether
# it still is.
#
# Base holds one file under two names, x and y.  Both sides rename x
# and neither touches y:
#
#   onto    x -> p     delta: lost {x}, gained {p}
#   off-of  x -> q     delta: lost {x}, gained {q}
#
# The name x is lost on BOTH sides and the gains disagree, so this
# conflicts.  Two people renamed the same link to two different
# things and no evidence anywhere says which they meant.
#
# What matters for the settlement is that the rule needs no
# antecedents to say so.  It does not ask "what succeeded x" on
# either side; it observes that one name left both deltas and that
# the two gained sets cannot be reconciled.  The old machinery
# reached the same verdict here through a pairing step it happened to
# be able to perform -- one lost, one gained on each side -- and the
# point of the fixture is that the verdict survives the pairing step
# being deleted.
#
# The verdict is the same before and after the settlement, like
# op6-straddle-split.  That is the intended result: a settlement that
# changed every verdict it touched would be a rewrite, and this rule
# was never the broken part.
#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  none; no directory changed
#   deltas:          onto lost {x} gained {p}; off-of lost {x} gained
#                    {q}; y is in neither delta
#   decisions:       none for the region.  It is quarantined and
#                    falls back to onto, so the pool keeps dnode
#                    10-100 under onto's names p and y
#   conflicts:       one, kind name
#   emission:        nothing; a quarantined region emits no edits
#
# CONFIDENCE.  The conflict KIND follows 5-rename-rename.tree, which
# is the same collision on a pool holding one name and which the
# engine has run.  The quarantine set follows the convention visible
# in that fixture and in 6-ambiguous-succession.tree: the paths named
# are the FALLBACK names, which is to say onto's.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: y

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: p = v0
      10-100: y

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: q = v0
      10-100: y

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect conflict name
expect quarantined /shared/p
expect quarantined /shared/y

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared
      10-100: p = v0
      10-100: y
